"Eindhoven University of Technology (https://pure.tue.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/319766521/Jansen_T.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; the book concerns placing polyominoes in a container.\nThe following sections specify what is meant by legacy software, introduce an example that will be used\nas the case study, formulate the research questions and approach, and provide an overview of this report.\n1.1 | The legacy software problem and polyform packing puzzles\nThe general term legacy software refers to any computer program that is in some way old. This could\nbe software that has been in development for a long time, or it could be an obsolete part of a larger\nsoftware system. Legacy code, the source code responsible for the legacy software, contributes to a growing\ntechnological debt. Increased maintenance costs form a liability for a company that depends on legacy\nsoftware to support their product or working environment.\nOne such piece of legacy software is Tom Verhoeff’s Pieces-in-Shape Puzzles, a collection of THINK Pascal\nprograms to solve polyform packing puzzles. A solved puzzle can be seen in Figure 1.1.1. These puzzles\nfind their origin in the world of polyominoes. Polyominoes are shapes made by connecting a number of\nunit squares along their edges. They were brought to the attention of the public by Golomb in 1953 [18].\nGolomb released a book containing theorems and open problems. One category of problems explored in\nthe book concerns placing polyominoes in a container. Given a set of polyominoes, is it possible to fit\nthem into a given container without overlap?\nFigure 1.1.1: A solved pentomino puzzle.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA352029 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A352029)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.7 years ago; Crawled: 6 months ago; John Mason, Illustrations of minimalist container polyominoes\n\n(Greetings from The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences!)\n\nA352029\n\na(n) is the number of minimalist polyominoes that can contain all n-ominoes.\n\n3\n\n1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 14, 204, 7\n\nOFFSET\n\n1,3\n\nCOMMENTS\n\na(n) is the number of free polyominoes that can contain all n-ominoes and that have size A327094(n), which is the minimum size for such a polyomino.\n\nFrom Peter Exley, Apr 06 2026: (Start)\n\na(8) = 7, proved by exhaustive SAT enumeration over all 36 valid bounding box partitions of 20 cells. The solver uses Counter-Example Guided Abstraction Refinement (CEGAR) with Glucose 4.2. All 7 minimalist containers are in bounding boxes 4 X 8 (6 solutions) and 5 X 8 (1 solution).\n\nThe sequence is not monotonic: a(7) = 204 >> a(8) = 7. This is caused by the exponential growth of A000105(n) (free n-ominoes) overwhelming the sub-quadratic growth of A327094(n) (minimum container size), creating extreme constraint density. At n=8, 369 pieces must fit in just 20 cells (density 18.5 pieces/cell vs 6.4 at n=7).\n\na(9) >= 111. All 111 known solutions are in bounding boxes 5 X 9 (10), 6 X 9 (97), 6 X 10 (1), and 7 X 9 (3).\n\nIndependently verified by two verifiers with disjoint code paths (matrix-based and complex-number orientations), both with local-optimality checks.\n\nLINKS\n\nTable of n, a(n) for n=1..8.\n\nPeter Exley, Solver code, figures, and data, GitHub.\n\nJohn Mason, Illustrations of minimalist container polyominoes\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nFrom Peter Exley, Apr 06 2026: (Start)\n\nFor n=8, the 7 minimalist 20-cell containers that hold all 369 free octominoes are:\n\nThe 6 containers with bounding box 4 X 8:\n\n# # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # #\n\n# # # # # # . . # # # # # # . . . # # # # # # .\n\n# # # . . . . . . . . # # # . . . # # # . . . .\n\n# # # . . . . . . . . # # # . . . # # # . . . .\n\n.\n\n# # # # # # . . # # # # # # . . . # # # # # # .\n\n# # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # #\n\n# # # . . . . . . . . # # # . . . # # # . . . .\n\n# # # . . . . . . . . # # # . . . # # # . . . .\n\nThe 1 container with bounding box 8 X 5:\n\n# # . . .\n\n. # # . .\n\n. # # # #\n\n. # # # .\n\n. # # # .\n\n# # # # .\n\n. # . . .\n\n. # . . .\n\n(End)\n\nCROSSREFS\n\nCf. A000105, A327094.\n\nSequence in context: A361815 A349564 A129409 * A025521 A305109 A372418\n\nAdjacent sequences: A352026 A352027 A352028 * A352030 A352031 A352032\n\nKEYWORD\n\nnonn,more,hard\n\nAUTHOR\n\nJohn Mason, Mar 04 2022\n\nEXTENSIONS\n\na(8) from Peter Exley, Apr 06 2026\n\nSTATUS\n\napproved--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroduction ▪ The Labyrinth of Polyominoes (https://minos.tessera.li/intro)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Packing Fitting a set of polyominoes tightly in some container.\n\n  *   * # Welcome to the Labyrinth.\n\nThis site is a encyclopedia and shrine to the polyominoes, figures made of squares joined by their edges:\n\nUnderlying their simple definition are complex and intricate shapes whose properties are still not fully known. There is no known formula for counting the number of polyominoes of a given size, and the ways that polyominoes tile and fit together are still being explored.\n\nPolyominoes have been featured in video games such as Tetris, to board games such as Blokus and Patchwork, to pencil puzzles such as Fillomino.\n\nThis site documents several interesting properties of polyominoes:\n\nCatalog A list of polyominoes up to octominoes with a summary of their properties. Symmetry An explanation of the different types of symmetries a polyomino can have. Classes A hierarchial categorization of polyominoes based on \"directedness\". Packing Fitting a set of polyominoes tightly in some container. Tiling Whether a single polyomino can completely fill the plane. Genealogy A \"family tree\" showing how polyominoes are built from simpler polyominoes.\n\nThis site is still a work in progress, and the world of polyominoes extends far beyond. So please, enjoy, learn, and get lost in the labyrinth.\n\nAnd please, watch out for the mino-taur.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDivision III (https://www.mathpentath.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/Script-for-Virtual-D3-Games-1.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; transparent dice container (depth-at least 1 ½ - 2 inches; ... Note:  Duplicate polyominoes should be placed on top of one another.\nJuggle™ \n \n \nPositioning of Gameboard, Polyominoes, Dice & Flag Card \n \n \n \n \n●\nwhite 20 x 30 foam board \n●\nDownload and print the following Labels:  Player A and Player B; Pentomino Legend; and 2 \nCoordinate Strip Labels (1-9) and (A-1) - one for each board.  Place each label as shown \nabove. \n●\ntransparent dice container (depth-at least 1 ½ - 2 inches; \ndiameter 2 ½ - 3 inches) \n●\nPlace polyominoes on top and between the 2 boards as shown. \nNote:  Duplicate polyominoes should be placed on top of one another. \n•     Host shakes dice container for Players. \n●\nPolyomino Selection:  participant describes polyomino by letter or geometric shape.  Host \npoints to corresponding polyomino. \n●\nPolyomino Placement:  participant uses coordinates to describe placement, e.g. (1,A), (1,B), \n1,C), (2,A), (2,C).Host uses pointer to indicate if correct, then places polyomino. \n●\nWild 6 Exact Value Method When Desired Polyomino is Not Available in Bank:  participant \ndescribes coordinate location of opponent’s piece.  Host points to and picks up piece. \nParticipant describes coordinate location of where to place on own board.  Host points and \nplaces as described.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nk-TRIANGULATIONS ON SURFACES (https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~reiner/REU/REU2024notes/Problem_5__k_Triangulations_on_Surfaces.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Cylindrical Polyominoes ... (i, j) represents a diagonal [αi, αj] on the universal